---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { SUBJECTS, UNLOCK_AFTER } from '../../site.ts';
import { subjectGuide } from '../../lib/educator.ts';
import { THINKING_MOVES, THINKING_MOVE_IDS } from '../../lib/thinking.ts';
import { levelBadge } from '../../lib/problem-html.ts';
import { href, absolute } from '../../lib/url.ts';

const description =
  'A guide for Alberta teachers and home-educating parents: how to use AB Edu to make learning fun, teach children to think, and adapt lessons for every child.';
const guides = SUBJECTS.filter((s) => subjectGuide(s.slug));
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'LearningResource',
    name: 'AB Edu guide for educators',
    description,
    url: absolute('educators/'),
    inLanguage: 'en-CA',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    learningResourceType: 'Teacher guide',
    audience: { '@type': 'EducationalAudience', educationalRole: 'teacher' },
  },
];
const stuck = [
  { say: 'What do you know for sure?', why: 'Starts from what they can do.' },
  { say: 'What is it really asking?', why: 'Most stuck children are stuck on the question, not the math.' },
  { say: 'Can you draw it?', why: 'A picture often shows what words hide.' },
  { say: 'What if the numbers were smaller?', why: 'Makes the idea easier to see.' },
  { say: 'What have you tried? What did you notice?', why: 'A wrong try is information.' },
];
const everyChild = [
  {
    title: 'Getting interested and staying with it',
    icon: '💛',
    items: [
      'Start from what your child loves. A hockey card collection can carry almost any math or data idea.',
      'Give real choices: which problem, what order, draw or explain.',
      'Keep sessions short, and stop while it’s still going well. One good Deep Think problem is a full day’s math.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Taking in the ideas',
    icon: '👁️',
    items: [
      'Use the site’s animations and visuals first, and replay them as often as needed.',
      'Read problems aloud together, or use your device’s read-aloud feature.',
      'Use real objects to show an idea before any symbols.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Showing what they know',
    icon: '🎤',
    items: [
      'Let them say it, draw it, build it or act it out. You can write for them.',
      'An explanation out loud counts as much as one on paper.',
      'Tick the self-check together on written problems. It’s a good talk, not a test.',
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base title="For teachers and parents" description={description} path="educators/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container ed">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="page-hero__eyebrow">For educators</p>
      <h1>You teach. We help.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__lede">
        This site doesn’t replace a teacher. It gives teachers and parents, including families who home-educate
        children with special needs, visuals, good problems and ideas that make learning something children want to do.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="ed-sections">
      <section class="card ed-card ed-card--hook">
        <h2><span aria-hidden="true">🧠</span> Why we care about thinking</h2>
        <p>
          A machine can now do the calculation or write the summary faster than any of us. What stays valuable is what
          comes before and after: working out what the question is, breaking a hard problem down, noticing what doesn’t
          fit, deciding what to believe, and imagining something new. Facts and procedures still matter, because
          they’re what we think <em>with</em>. But drilling them isn’t the goal, and it isn’t much fun.
        </p>
        <p>So on this site, hard doesn’t mean bigger numbers. It means more thinking.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="ed-heading"><span aria-hidden="true">🪜</span> How practice is built</h2>
        <div class="grid">
          <article class="card ed-card">
            <Fragment set:html={levelBadge(1)} />
            <p>One idea, one step, a quick win. Builds confidence and shows whether the lesson landed.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card ed-card">
            <Fragment set:html={levelBadge(2)} />
            <p>Read a short story, table or map, then decide what to do. Solving {UNLOCK_AFTER} unlocks the next level, and anyone can peek ahead.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card ed-card">
            <Fragment set:html={levelBadge(3)} />
            <p>Investigate, design, find the flaw, defend a choice. Friendly numbers, real thinking. Struggling here is the point.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="ed-note">Nothing needs knowledge above the grade. Every problem traces to Alberta’s program of studies, and every problem has hints, a worked solution and feedback that explains the thinking.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="card ed-card">
        <h2><span aria-hidden="true">🛟</span> When a child is stuck, try not to rescue</h2>
        <p>Being stuck is where the learning happens. Wait a little longer than feels comfortable, then ask one of these instead of explaining:</p>
        <dl class="ed-stuck">
          {
            stuck.map((s) => (
              <div>
                <dt>“{s.say}”</dt>
                <dd>{s.why}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="ed-heading"><span aria-hidden="true">🗣️</span> The moves children learn</h2>
        <p class="ed-note">After solving a problem, a child sees which thinking move they just used. Use the same words at the dinner table: “You worked backwards there!”</p>
        <ul class="ed-moves">
          {
            THINKING_MOVE_IDS.filter((m) => m !== 'recall' && m !== 'apply').map((m) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{THINKING_MOVES[m].name}</strong>
                <span>{THINKING_MOVES[m].blurb}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="subjects">
        <h2 class="ed-heading"><span aria-hidden="true">📚</span> Guides by subject</h2>
        <div class="grid">
          {
            guides.map((s) => (
              <a class="card concept-card" href={href(`grade-5/${s.slug}/educators/`)}>
                <span class="concept-card__body">
                  <span class="concept-card__title">{s.name}</span>
                  <span class="concept-card__sub">Routines, plus notes for every topic</span>
                </span>
                <Icon name="arrow" size={18} />
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <p class="ed-note">Every topic page also links to its own notes: a hook, off-screen activities, questions to ask, common ideas children have, and ways to adapt it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="every-child">
        <h2 class="ed-heading"><span aria-hidden="true">🌱</span> A guide for every child</h2>
        <p class="ed-note">
          These follow the three principles of <a href="https://udlguidelines.cast.org/" rel="noopener">Universal Design for Learning</a>: build in choice from
          the start, so fewer children need to be treated as exceptions. Each topic’s notes add specifics.
        </p>
        <div class="grid">
          {
            everyChild.map((g) => (
              <article class="card ed-card">
                <h3><span aria-hidden="true">{g.icon}</span> {g.title}</h3>
                <ul>{g.items.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="card ed-card ed-built-in">
          <h3>Already built into the site</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Lessons and practice work with a keyboard and still fit at 400% zoom. Automated accessibility checks pass on every page, but people who use screen readers every day haven’t tried the site yet.</li>
            <li>Dark mode, a font designed for readability, and reduced motion if your device asks for it.</li>
            <li>No timers, no accounts and no scores for others to see. Wrong answers get an explanation, not a penalty.</li>
            <li>Hints and solutions are always available, and levels never fully lock.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="muted ed-note">These are ideas to try, not medical or professional advice. A child’s own team knows them best.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="card ed-card" id="home">
        <h2><span aria-hidden="true">🏡</span> Home education in Alberta</h2>
        <p>
          Alberta parents can direct a home education program for Grades 1 to 12, either supervised by a school authority (which brings funding and two
          evaluations a year) or as notification only (no funding). Some students have an identified need for additional supports, and the Association of
          Independent Schools and Colleges of Alberta helps home education families find resources, workshops and assessments. See
          <a href="https://www.alberta.ca/home-education" rel="noopener">Alberta’s home education page</a> for the current rules. This site can support a program,
          but it isn’t a full program, and it isn’t endorsed by Alberta Education.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="ed-note">
        <h2 class="ed-heading ed-heading--small">Where these ideas come from</h2>
        <ul class="muted">
          <li><a href="https://udlguidelines.cast.org/" rel="noopener">CAST: Universal Design for Learning Guidelines 3.0</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://open.alberta.ca/publications/9780778586012" rel="noopener">Alberta Education: Making a Difference, on differentiated instruction</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.alberta.ca/inclusive-education" rel="noopener">Government of Alberta: inclusive education</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://nrich.maths.org/7701" rel="noopener">NRICH: using low-threshold, high-ceiling tasks</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.buildingthinkingclassrooms.com/about-btc" rel="noopener">Peter Liljedahl: Building Thinking Classrooms</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
